===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[level#Noun|level]] or [[rank#Noun|rank]] in an [[organization]], [[profession]], or [[society]].
#* {{quote-book|en|editors=C. F. Hollander and H. A. Becker|chapter=Scenario A: The Reference Scenario|title=Growing Old in the Future: Scenarios on Health and Ageing 1984–2000: Scenario-report, Commissioned by the Steering Committee on Future Health Scenarios|location=Dordrecht|publisher={{w|Martinus Nijhoff Publishers}}|year=1987|section=section 2.5.3 (GP Care)|page=85|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PWmvBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA85|doi=10.1007/978-94-009-3309-5|isbn=978-0-89838-869-5|passage=Other important functions performed by the GP [general practitioner] are those of referring patients to other (health) care facilities and acting as contact person for other providers of aid, both for other facilities in first '''echelon''' care and with respect to second '''echelon''' care (outpatient care and treatment in hospital).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alfred Kuo-liang Ho|chapter=[[w:Deng Xiaoping|Deng]]’s Political Reforms|title=China’s Reforms and Reformers|location=Westport, Conn.|publisher=Praeger Publishers, {{w|Greenwood Publishing Group}}|year=2004|page=120|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uJ7UoPJ9hGYC&pg=PA120|isbn=978-0-275-96080-3|passage=Officials in China are divided into three '''echelons''', or generations. The first '''echelon''' joined the Communist Party in the 1920s, soon after the party was founded. {{...}} The second '''echelon''' joined the Communist Party before the founding of the People's Republic of China in 1949. The party was then still struggling, and many of the second '''echelon''' died during the wars. They were again true patriots. The third '''echelon''' joined the Communist Party after the founding of the People's Republic.}}
# {{lb|en|cycling}} A [[line#Noun|line]] of [[rider]]s [[seek]]ing [[maximum]] [[drafting#Noun|drafting]] in a [[crosswind]], resulting in a [[diagonal]] line across the [[road]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Journal of the American Association for Health, Physical Education, and Recreation|location=[Washington, D.C.]|publisher=American Association for Health, Physical Education, and Recreation|year=1972|volume=43|page=22|column=2|oclc=7980094|passage=In an '''echelon''', in which several cyclists are sitting in on one another, each rider takes his turn of about 200 meters at the front before dropping to the rear.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Fred Matheny|title=Bicycling Magazine’s Complete Guide to Riding and Racing Techniques|location=Emmaus, Pa.|publisher=[[w:Rodale, Inc.|Rodale Press]]|year=1989|page=99|isbn=978-0-87857-804-7|passage=Cyclists in an '''echelon''' take up more room in the traffic lane than they do when riding single or double file.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} A [[formation]] of [[troops]], [[ship#Noun|ships]], etc., in [[diagonal]] [[parallel]] [[row#Noun|rows]]. {{defdate|from late 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=“a royalist”|title=Sketches of the War of the French in Spain in the Year 1823|magazine=The United Service Journal and Naval and Military Magazine|location=London|publisher=Published for {{w|Henry Colburn}}, by [[w:Richard Bentley (publisher)|Richard Bentley]], {{w|New Burlington Street}}|month=October|year=1833|volume_plain=part III|issue=59|page=183|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jhoUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA183|oclc=933313799|passage=The troops selected by his Royal Highness for this daring exploit, consisted of the war battalions of the 3d, 6th, and 7th regiments of the Royal Guard, forming the first '''echelon''', {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ed. [Édouard] De La Barre Duparcq|author2=[[w:George Washington Cullum|George W[ashington] Cullum]], transl.|chapter=Infantry Formation and Tactics|title=Elements of Military Art and History: Comprising the History and Tactics of the Separate Arms; the Combination of the Arms; and the Minor Operations of War|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:David Van Nostrand|D[avid] Van Nostrand]], 192 [[w:Broadway (Manhattan)|Broadway]]|year=1863|page=84|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=o_9WAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA84|oclc=|passage=The order in '''echelons''' is favorable for attack, because it readily conforms to the nature of the ground, and does not necessitate engaging more than a part of the forces; it is adopted for the purpose of attacking a particular point of the enemy's line.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Winston Spencer Churchill|authorlink=Winston Churchill|chapter=The Battle of Omdurman: September 2, 1898|editor=[[w:Frank Rhodes (British Army officer)|F[rancis] Rhodes]]|title=[[w:The River War|The River War: An Historical Account of the Reconquest of the Soudan [...] In Two Volumes]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longmans, Green, and Co.]], 89 {{w|Paternoster Row}}|year=1899|volume=II|pages=145–146|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/1899RiverWarVol2/1899%20River%20War%20Vol%202#page/n169/mode/1up|oclc=457694485|passage=As soon as the infantry had replenished their ammunition, they wheeled to the left in '''échelon''' of brigades, and began to march towards Surgham ridge. The movements of a great force are slow. It was not desirable that the British division, which led the '''échelon''', should remain in the low ground north of Surgham—where it was commanded, had no field of fire, and could see nothing—and accordingly both these brigades moved forward almost together to occupy the crest of the ridge.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/mar/28/the-drone-operators-who-halted-the-russian-armoured-vehicles-heading-for-kyiv|title=The drone operators who halted Russian convoy headed for Kyiv|quotee=Yaroslav Honchar|date=2022-03-28|author=Julian Borger|work=The Guardian|passage=“The first '''echelon''' of the Russian force was stuck without heat, without oil, without bombs and without gas. And it all happened because of the work of 30 people,” Honchar said.}}

