#* {{quote-book|en|author=François-Xavier Garneau|authorlink=François-Xavier Garneau|author2=Andrew Bell, transl.|chapter=Battle of Carillon (Ticonderoga). 1758.|title=History of Canada, from the Time of Its Discovery till the Union Year (1840–1): Translated from “L’Histoire du Canada” of F. X. Garneau, Esq., and Accompanied with Illustrative Notes, etc., etc. [...] In Three Volumes|location=Montreal, Que.|publisher=Printed and published by John Lovell, St. Nicholas Street|year=1860|volume=II|page=205|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/historycanadafr00garngoog#page/n211/mode/1up|oclc=937926093|passage=July 1, Montcalm made a movement in advance, '''''echeloning''''' his troops from Fort Carillon to the foot of Lake George, to curb the enemy, and obstruct their landing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Earl F[rederick] Ziemke|authorlink=Earl F. Ziemke|chapter=Offensives on Both Flanks—the South Flank|title=Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East|series=Army Historical Series|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Army Center of Military History|Office of the Chief of Military History]], [[w:United States Army|U.S. Army]]|year=1968|oclc=1175695|location2=Washington, D.C.|publisher2=[[w:United States Army Center of Military History|Center of Military History]], United States Army|series2=CMH Pub|seriesvolume2=30–5–1|year2=2002|page2=225|pageurl2=https://archive.org/stream/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast-nsia/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast#page/n237/mode/1up|oclc2=781285997|passage=Behind the 17th Panzer Division the corps '''echeloned''' the main force of its other division, the 16th Panzer Division.}}

