===Adjective===
{{examples|examples=An example of a 4×6 matrix in row echelon form (the <math>*</math> indicate the nonzero pivots, and the <math>\cdot</math> indicate entries which may be zero or nonzero):
<math>
\left[ \begin{array}{cccccc}
* & \cdot & \cdot & \cdot & \cdot & \cdot \\
0 & * & \cdot & \cdot & \cdot & \cdot \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & * & \cdot \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0
\end{array} \right]
</math>}}
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|linear algebra}} Of a [[matrix]]: having undergone {{w|Gaussian elimination}} with the result that the leading [[coefficient]] or [[pivot#Noun|pivot]] (that is, the first [[nonzero]] [[number#Noun|number]] from the left) of a nonzero [[row#Noun|row]] is to the right of the pivot of the row above it, giving rise to a [[stepped#Adjective|stepped]] [[appearance]] in the matrix.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Malcolm Pemberton|author2=Nicholas Rau|chapter=Systems of Linear Equations|title=Mathematics for Economists: An Introductory Textbook|location=Manchester; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Manchester University Press}}|year=2001|section=section 12.1 (Echelon Matrices)|page=204|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rJ5d7DW2t4UC&pg=PA204|isbn=978-0-7190-3340-7|passage=An '''''echelon''' matrix'' is a matrix, not necessarily square, with the following two properties: (i) There is at least one non-zero entry; rows consisting entirely of zeros, if any, lie below rows with at least one non-zero entry. (ii) In each non-zero row after the first, the left-most non-zero entry lies to the right of the left-most non-zero entry in the preceding row. {{...}} In each of the non-zero rows of an '''echelon''' matrix, the left-most non-zero entry is called the ''pivot'', {{...}}}}

